# CODEOWNERS — the hand-written fallback. There is now a generator, and it is the better path:
#
#   python3 coordination/tools/ownership.py --map ORCH=@you -o .github/CODEOWNERS
#   python3 coordination/tools/ownership.py --map-file owners.json --check   # CI drift gate
#
# It reads the same OWNERSHIP.md table, so the two files cannot describe the same fact
# differently. Keep this template for the case where you want to hand-write the file, and as
# documentation of what the generated output means.
#
# Either way: replace every <...>_GITHUB_USER placeholder with a real username or team before
# this does anything useful.
#
# GitHub uses the LAST matching rule, not the most specific one — order general to specific,
# top to bottom, the same way you'd read OWNERSHIP.md's "exclusive zones" table from general
# rule to override.

# Default owner — reviews anything not claimed by a more specific rule below.
*                              @<ORCH_GITHUB_USER>

# One line per role's exclusive zone (mirror OWNERSHIP.md's "Exclusive zones" table exactly —
# same paths, same owners, so the two files can't silently drift apart):
# /path/owned/by/role/**       @<ROLE_GITHUB_USER>

coordination/**                @<ORCH_GITHUB_USER>
.github/**                     @<ORCH_GITHUB_USER>

# ── Read before you rely on this file ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# CODEOWNERS only becomes an actual review GATE (not just a hint) once branch protection has
# "Require review from Code Owners" turned on for the target branch — see the skill's references/setup.md
# §6 for where to click. Without that setting this file just pre-fills the reviewer list on a
# PR; nothing stops a merge without their approval.
#
# It also only means something if the listed owners are DIFFERENT GitHub accounts with actual
# write access to the repo. In a single-maintainer repo, listing yourself as every zone's owner
# is honest documentation of intent (this is who's responsible for this path) but is not a real
# multi-person gate — you can always approve your own PR. Don't present it as enforcement it
# isn't; see the skill's references/git-github-rails.md for the fuller version of this caveat.
